\ Benchmark: raw compiled<->interpreted boundary cost per quotation call.
\
\ The prior AOT-linter finding attributed the slowness to the
\ compiled<->interpreter boundary being far more expensive per call. This
\ isolates exactly that crossing: a quotation retrieved from a hash at runtime
\ (the lexer's `match: @get ... call` shape, which the AOT compiler cannot
\ inline) is called in a tight loop with a trivial body (`1 +`). No generic
\ dispatch, no string work -- just the per-crossing cost.
\
\ Structured so the loop compiles under runtime-image AOT while the dynamic
\ `call` in `do-call` is the single word that falls back to the interpreter --
\ the same shape as the real lexer's per-token rule call.
\
\   AOT ~= interpreter  -> the boundary thunk itself is cheap; the prior
\                          attribution is wrong and the cost is elsewhere.
\   AOT >> interpreter  -> the boundary crossing is the per-call cost the
\                          prior finding named (quantified here in isolation).

\ Hash holding a quotation, retrieved at runtime so the call stays dynamic.
qholder: ( -- hash ) const [ H{ step: [ 1 + ] } ] ;

iters: ( -- fixnum ) const [ 2000000 ] ;

\ The dynamic boundary crossing: pull a quotation from the hash and call it.
do-call: ( n -- n ) [ qholder step: @get call ] ;

run-loop: ( -- ) [ iters [ 0 do-call drop ] times ] ;

\ warm
0 do-call drop

"boundary-raw iters:      " print iters .
"boundary-raw elapsed_ns: " print
[ run-loop ] benchmark "elapsed_ns" @get .
